    {
      "id": 29,
      "title": "Health",
      "type": "stat",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 4,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 104
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "prometheus"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "expr": "zfs_pool_health{pool=\"$pool\"}",
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "mappings": [
            {
              "type": "value",
              "options": {
                "0": {
                  "text": "ONLINE",
                  "color": "green"
                },
                "1": {
                  "text": "DEGRADED",
                  "color": "orange"
                },
                "2": {
                  "text": "FAULTED",
                  "color": "red"
                },
                "3": {
                  "text": "OFFLINE",
                  "color": "red"
                },
                "4": {
                  "text": "UNAVAIL",
                  "color": "red"
                },
                "5": {
                  "text": "REMOVED",
                  "color": "red"
                },
                "6": {
                  "text": "SUSPENDED",
                  "color": "red"
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "description": "NAME\n    Health \u2014 aggregate pool state\n\nDESCRIPTION\n    The single-value state ZFS assigns to the pool. ZFS classifies a\n    pool as one of: ONLINE, DEGRADED, FAULTED, OFFLINE, REMOVED, or\n    UNAVAIL. The state is the same value `zpool status` reports.\n\nEQUIVALENT IN OTHER TOOLS\n    In a non-ZFS Linux storage stack there is no single equivalent.\n    The same information is distributed across:\n        mdadm --detail            array layout, member state, sync\n                                  progress (software RAID only).\n        lvs / vgs / pvs           volume manager state.\n        cryptsetup status         per-mapping encryption state.\n        dmsetup status            device-mapper target state.\n        fsck output / dmesg       filesystem-detected errors.\n    None of those tools reports block-level checksum errors, because\n    none of those layers performs end-to-end checksumming. A silent\n    data corruption (bit rot, controller bug, cable error) that\n    bypasses RAID parity is invisible to that stack until an\n    application reads the data and notices it is wrong.\n\n    ZFS combines volume management, redundancy, encryption, and\n    end-to-end checksums into one subsystem with one reported state.\n\nSOURCE\n    zfs_exporter polls `zpool list -H -o name,health` every scrape\n    interval (15s default) and emits:\n        zfs_pool_health{pool=\"...\"}\n    Numeric values map to:\n        0   ONLINE\n        1   DEGRADED\n        2   FAULTED\n        3   OFFLINE\n        4   REMOVED\n        5   UNAVAIL\n\nFIELDS\n    ONLINE      Normal operation. All vdevs reachable and healthy.\n    DEGRADED    A vdev failure has occurred but redundancy is\n                tolerating it. Service continues. Investigate which\n                vdev (zpool status) and replace before redundancy is\n                exhausted.\n    FAULTED     The pool cannot be opened in its current state.\n                Service is down for any dataset on this pool.\n    OFFLINE     Operator explicitly took a vdev offline.\n    REMOVED     A vdev was physically removed at runtime.\n    UNAVAIL     A vdev is missing on import (disk gone, path moved).\n\nCAVEATS\n    This panel reports the pool's overall state only. Per-vdev\n    detail, specific error counts, and recovery progress are not\n    here. Use the zed events panel (#12) for individual vdev\n    transitions and zpool status(8) for full per-vdev state.\n\nSEE ALSO\n    Panel #12 \u2014 zed events. Live transitions of vdev state.\n    zpool(8) status \u2014 full pool state as a tree.\n    /etc/zfs/zpool.cache \u2014 last-known layout.\n    Documentation/admin-guide/zfs-tuning.rst \u2014 Linux kernel notes.\n"
    },
