    {
      "id": 35,
      "title": "ARC Hit Rate",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 7,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 108
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "prometheus"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "expr": "100 * sum(rate(zfs_arcstat{metric=\"hits\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(zfs_arcstat{metric=~\"hits|misses\"}[5m]))",
          "refId": "A",
          "legendFormat": "ARC hit %"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percent",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100
        }
      },
      "description": "NAME\n    ARC Hit Rate \u2014 read requests served from the in-kernel cache\n\nDESCRIPTION\n    Percentage of read requests served from the ZFS Adaptive\n    Replacement Cache (ARC) without reaching the underlying vdevs,\n    computed over a 5-minute rolling window.\n\nEQUIVALENT IN OTHER TOOLS\n    The Linux page cache, used by ext4/xfs/btrfs, does not expose an\n    equivalent hit-rate metric by default. The closest approximation\n    in a non-ZFS stack is /usr/share/bcc/tools/cachestat \u2014 an eBPF\n    tool that traces page-cache events in real time. Two structural\n    differences make the numbers not directly comparable:\n\n        Eviction policy. The Linux page cache is LRU (Least Recently\n        Used). ARC is MRU+MFU (Most Recently Used + Most Frequently\n        Used, with a ghost list to detect repeated misses). For\n        repeated random-access workloads \u2014 database working sets,\n        for example \u2014 ARC retains hot data through scans that would\n        evict the page cache.\n\n        Scope. The page cache covers all filesystems' read I/O. ARC\n        covers ZFS read I/O specifically. The two can coexist; ZFS\n        sets primarycache=metadata on datasets where the application\n        has its own cache (Postgres shared_buffers) to avoid\n        double-caching.\n\n    No Splunk, Loki, or Dynatrace panel reports ARC hit rate by\n    default. Some Splunk apps include cachestat output if it was\n    scraped to a log file, in which case the data is sampled and\n    delayed, not real-time.\n\nSOURCE\n    zfs_exporter reads /proc/spl/kstat/zfs/arcstats every scrape\n    interval (15s default) and exposes per-counter gauges:\n        zfs_arcstat{metric=\"hits\"}\n        zfs_arcstat{metric=\"misses\"}\n    Query:\n        100 * rate(hits[5m]) / rate(hits+misses[5m])\n\nFIELDS\n    > 95 %     Working set fits comfortably in ARC.\n    80 - 95 %  Healthy with occasional capacity misses.\n    < 80 %     Working set exceeds ARC capacity. Consider larger\n               primarycache, more system RAM allocated to ZFS, or\n               L2ARC if read pattern justifies it.\n    Sudden drops to near-0 % indicate either a cache flush (e.g.,\n    primarycache property change on a busy dataset) or a large new\n    dataset displacing the working set.\n\nCAVEATS\n    Hit rate is system-wide, not per-dataset. A high overall rate\n    can mask a poor rate on one specific dataset. The arcstats\n    counters are accumulative from boot; if the ZFS module is\n    reloaded, the rate calculation resets.\n\nSEE ALSO\n    Panel #8 \u2014 ARC Size vs Target. Shows current cache size and\n        how it compares to the configured target.\n    /proc/spl/kstat/zfs/arcstats \u2014 raw counters.\n    /usr/share/bcc/tools/cachestat \u2014 page cache equivalent.\n    zfs(8) primarycache property documentation.\n    Sun Solaris ARC paper (Megiddo & Modha, 2003) \u2014 original design.\n"
    },
    {
